{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2403.07904, why are third-party audits by researchers and civil society vital for an EU AI audit ecosystem, and what challenges do they face?", "answer": "Third-party audits are vital because they provide oversight, compliance, and include affected communities, but they face challenges such as lack of technical capacity, risk of leaking secrets, and resource constraints.", "evidence": [{"page": 20, "quote": "We argue that for such an ecosystem, third-party audits by researchers and civil society are a vital component to fulfill the characteristics of the ecosystem and thus also provide both oversight and compliance through first-party audits."}, {"page": 26, "quote": "Third-party audits have some drawbacks. These auditors may lack the technical knowledge or capacity to thoroughly research a system. There is also a risk of revealing secret information or company secrets, which can be prevented by including nondisclosure clauses."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2403.07904, how does the AIA's lack of data access provisions for third parties impact the ability to audit high-risk AI systems?", "answer": "The AIA's lack of data access provisions limits third-party audits to process-based compliance, making it impossible to verify reports without additional data access.", "evidence": [{"page": 16, "quote": "It was shown that the AIA does not encompass data or model access provisions for third parties, even in the context of high-risk systems. Nonetheless, in the subsequent discussion, we will explore the potential access levels and, consequently, the knowledge accessible to third parties concerning AI technologies."}, {"page": 17, "quote": "Document audits are often broad sets of information that can be analysed in different ways [60] and ensure the possiblity for third-party impact evaluations or conformity assessments as intended by the AIA. However, only process-based compliance is feasible through document audits, there are no standards for most of these reports and they are impossible to verify if no other data access is provided [60]."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2403.07904, what are the characteristics of an AI audit ecosystem?", "answer": "An AI audit ecosystem is open, dynamic, and partially self-organizing, involving independent yet interdependent auditors and audit types.", "evidence": [{"page": 20, "quote": "We define an AI audit ecosystem as an open dynamic partial self-organizing community of hierarchically independent, yet interdependent heterogeneous auditors and audit types77 . The characteristics are described by the following:\n\n- open, meaning that it interferes with other accountability processes, such as reporting, enforcement, and harm incident reporting\n\n- dynamic over time (and thereby adaptive to new forms of AI systems, which may have to be audited differently) and over stakeholders (it must involve potentially affected communities and users)\n\n- partial self-organization of auditors in an enabling environment78 and a promotion of sharing audit information through interaction between auditors (formal and informal)"}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2403.07904, what is meant by 'process access' in the context of ML algorithm audits?", "answer": "'Process access' refers to access to frameworks, checklists, model cards, datasheets, method papers, and technical reports, enabling document audits like algorithmic risk assessments.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "| Process access                               | Frameworks, check- lists, model Cards, datasheets, method paper, technical reports                       | Checklists for explainability, robustness, fairness and pri- vacy                                                                                                                         | Document audits (al- gorithmic risk assess- ment and algorithmic impact evaluations) | Audit Austrian AMS algorithm[5]                                                                                                      |"}]},
  {"question": "Which audit of a welfare fraud ADM in Rotterdam involved direct control over model parameters, according to arXiv 2403.07904?", "answer": "The audit of Rotterdam's welfare fraud ADM involved parameter control and learning goal access.", "evidence": [{"page": 18, "quote": "| Parameter control and learn- ing goal access | Learning procedure and target L, param- eters φ, L(fφ(x)) can be retrained of                            | Stability of explanations, stability analysis, stability bias metrics, function- ality stealing, model com- plexity, stress-testing, trade- off of bias and loss metric, model extraction | Retraining of the ac- tual model with dif- ferent parameters                         | Audit of the Rotter- dams' welfare fraud ADM[20, 21]                                                                                 |"}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2403.07904, how does the text describe the risk of companies manipulating audit standards to their advantage?", "answer": "The text explains that companies may 'game the system' by adhering to audit metrics while pursuing other incentives, leading to decoupling from intended audit purposes.", "evidence": [{"page": 22, "quote": "Gaming the system is connected to Goodhart's law, which is usually paraphrased as \"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.\" Thus, behaviors are created to achieve the metrics while pursuing other business incentives simultaneously. Methods to game the system emerge to avoid implementing the desired behavior measured by the metrics [70]."}]}
]}
